.TH BUFFER_PUT_QUOTED 3 "15 Mar 2023" "CB" "Library Functions"

.SH NAME
buffer_put_quoted \- Put quoted data into a buffer

.SH SYNOPSIS
.IB #include <lib/buffer.h>
.IB #include <lib/uint32.h>
.IB #include <lib/fmt.h>

.IB int
.IB buffer_put_quoted (buffer* b, const char* x, size_t n);

.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBbuffer_put_quoted\fR function puts quoted data into a buffer. It iterates over the input data, and for each character, it checks if the character is a control character (less than 0x20). If it is, it converts the character to a quoted representation using the \fBfmt_8long\fR function and appends it to the buffer. If it is not a control character, it simply appends the character to the buffer.

The function takes the following parameters:
.IB buffer*
A pointer to the buffer structure.
.IB const char*
The input data to be put into the buffer.
.IB size_t
The length of the input data.

The function returns the number of bytes put into the buffer.

The \fBbuffer_put_quoted\fR function is intended to be used to put quoted data into a buffer, such as when formatting data for output.

.SH EXAMPLES
The following example demonstrates how to use the \fBbuffer_put_quoted\fR function to put quoted data into a buffer:

.nf
#include <stdio.h>
#include <lib/buffer.h>
#include <lib/uint32.h>
#include <lib/fmt.h>

int main() {
    buffer* b = buffer_new();
    const char* data = "Hello, world!";
    size_t n = strlen(data);

    int bytes = buffer_put_quoted(b, data, n);
    if(bytes < 0) {
        perror("buffer_put_quoted");
        buffer_free(b);
        return 1;
    }

    buffer_free(b);
    return 0;
}
.nef